---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import StaticFigure from '@/components/figures/registry';
import { corpus } from '@/data/corpus';
import { url } from '@/lib/url';
import sources from '../../scripts/watch/sources.json';

const srcByCat = sources.sources.reduce<Record<string, string[]>>((acc, s) => {
  (acc[s.category] ??= []).push(s.name);
  return acc;
}, {});
const catLabel: Record<string, string> = { lab: 'Frontier labs', research: 'Research and evaluation', newsletter: 'Expert newsletters', policy: 'Government and regulators', economy: 'Economics', media: 'Journalism, podcasts, video', community: 'Community' };
---

<BaseLayout title="Method" description="Why the path is built the way it is: backward design from the frontier corpus, labor-market evidence, the science of learning, and how the daily watcher decides what is significant." active="method">
  <header class="aura aura-t7" style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)">
    <div class="container-read" style="padding-block:clamp(48px,8vw,104px) clamp(32px,5vw,56px)">
      <div class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--fg)">Method</div>
      <h1 style="margin-top:12px">Why this path, in this order.</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="margin-top:16px;color:var(--fg);opacity:.8">
        Three foundations: a map worked backward from the documents you will read, evidence on how AI is changing work, and the science of learning.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="container-read prose" style="padding-block:48px 24px">
    <p>
      The goal: open any Anthropic or OpenAI paper, system card, or AI policy text and answer four questions. What is claimed? How was it measured? How strong is the evidence? What follows?
    </p>

    <h2 id="curation">Learn from the original teachers and sources</h2>
    <p>External material carries the main instruction: animated explanations, visual essays, interactive tools, and original research or policy documents. Each module has an ordered selection, a specific focus for each source, and a task that brings the ideas back to your own reasoning. Supporting notes and local simulations are available when you need them.</p>
    <p>At least 70% of each module’s suggested core study time is allocated to the external selections. These are study budgets, including pauses and exploration, not exact media runtimes. Finishing a video or opening a link does not demonstrate mastery; the checks still ask you to use what you learned.</p>
    <p>For practical AI use, start with <a href={url('/learn/ai-fluency/')}>the 4D learning sequence</a>. It directs you to the <a href="https://academy.claude.com/courses/ai-fluency-framework-foundations">official AI Fluency course</a> by Rick Dakan, Joseph Feller, and Anthropic. The framework is by Dakan and Feller. Their course materials carry a <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 license</a>; our links take you to the originals. WhatisAI’s companion activities are independent and do not confer the provider’s course badge.</p>

    <h2 id="backward">1. Designed backward from the corpus</h2>
    <p>
      We started from the {corpus.length} documents in the <a href={url('/library/')}>library</a> and asked what a reader needs to evaluate each one, not just skim it. The answers became the ten tracks.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reading <em>Scaling Monosemanticity</em> takes vector geometry, superposition, the residual stream, and dictionary learning. Reading a system card takes benchmark statistics and dangerous-capability evaluation. Reading the EU AI Act's rules for general-purpose models takes knowing what training compute is and why the threshold sits at 10<sup>25</sup> operations. Module prerequisites encode these dependencies.
    </p>
    <p>
      The deepest dependency is math. Frontier documents assume five ideas: vectors and similarity, probability, gradient descent, statistical uncertainty, and log scales. Track I teaches them from zero, each in service of an AI concept.
    </p>

    <h2 id="labor">2. Shaped by the labor market</h2>
    <p>The evidence points one way: toward judgment about AI, not just use of it.</p>

    <h3>Exposure is broad</h3>
    <p>
      About 80% of US workers could have at least 10% of their tasks affected by language models, and about 19% could see half or more, with exposure rising with education and wages. Exposure measures where change is possible, not what happens.
    </p>

    <h3>Use is concentrated, and mostly augments</h3>
    <p>
      Anthropic's Economic Index maps millions of real conversations to occupational tasks. In early 2025, software and math made up over a third of use, and 57% of use augmented work while 43% automated it. Later editions through 2026 show use spreading to more tasks and to lower-wage work.
    </p>
    <StaticFigure id="aei-bars" caption="Claude conversations by occupational category, February 2025. Source: Anthropic Economic Index." />

    <h3>The productivity frontier is jagged</h3>
    <p>
      Support agents resolved 14% more issues per hour with AI, and novices 34% more. Consultants working inside GPT-4's abilities finished 12% more tasks, 25% faster. On a task just outside them, they were 19 points <em>less</em> likely to be right. Experienced developers in a 2025 trial were 19% <em>slower</em> with AI, while believing they were faster.
    </p>
    <StaticFigure id="forest" caption="Effects from five studies. Measures differ, so compare directions, not magnitudes. Sources: Brynjolfsson et al.; Noy & Zhang; Dell'Acqua et al.; Peng et al.; METR." />

    <h3>Entry-level work changes first</h3>
    <p>
      Payroll data covering millions of workers shows employment for 22 to 25 year olds in the most exposed jobs falling relative to peers since late 2022, by about 19% as of the August 2026 update. Experienced workers in the same jobs held steady. The change came through slower hiring, not layoffs. The authors call the pattern descriptive, not proof of cause.
    </p>
    <p>
      The macro picture is contested. Task-based estimates (Acemoglu, 2024) imply modest gains over a decade; others expect far larger ones. The International AI Safety Report 2026 records that economists still disagree.
    </p>

    <h3>What this means for the path</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Judgment is the scarce skill.</strong> On a jagged frontier, what matters is knowing which side a task falls on. Hence the weight on evaluation (Track V) and a confidence wager on every question.</li>
      <li><strong>Book knowledge is the most exposed.</strong> The path trains reasoning with evidence over memorizing facts.</li>
      <li><strong>Directing AI beats delegating to it.</strong> Knowing how models work is what makes skilled direction possible.</li>
      <li><strong>Governance is now a profession.</strong> The EU AI Act, California's SB 53, and lab safety frameworks need people who can read a system card and a statute. Tracks VI and VIII are for them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="learning">3. Built on the science of learning</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Mastery learning.</strong> Advance by showing understanding. In Bloom's studies, tutored mastery learning beat conventional teaching by about two standard deviations. Each module ends in a check with an 80% bar.</li>
      <li><strong>Retrieval practice.</strong> Recalling beats rereading. Quick checks appear mid-lesson.</li>
      <li><strong>Spacing.</strong> Spread-out practice beats cramming. Answered questions return at expanding intervals (SM-2 family).</li>
      <li><strong>Calibration.</strong> Knowing what you know can be trained. Every answer carries a wager, scored with the Brier score forecasters use.</li>
      <li><strong>Active learning.</strong> Across 225 studies it cut STEM failure rates by about a third. The interactives let you manipulate each mechanism directly.</li>
      <li><strong>Adaptive testing.</strong> Placement binary-searches each track and finds your level in about three questions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="watcher">4. How the watcher works</h2>
    <p>Every morning it reads {sources.sources.length} sources:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Sources</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {Object.entries(srcByCat).map(([cat, names]) => <tr><td>{catLabel[cat] ?? cat}</td><td>{names.join(', ')}</td></tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Remembers.</strong> Only never-seen items count as news.</li>
      <li><strong>Measures trends.</strong> Compares each of about thirty topics' share of this week's coverage with the previous four weeks. See the <a href={url('/trends/')}>trend radar</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Scores stories.</strong> Clusters related reports and scores each story on source authority, corroboration, impact markers, and engagement.</li>
      <li><strong>Publishes only when it matters.</strong> Most days nothing clears the bar. When a story does, Claude reads the primary sources, checks the facts, and writes a short signal linked to the relevant modules. Without Claude, a stricter bar applies and the signal is excerpted, never paraphrased.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>It costs nothing.</strong> Public feeds, free GitHub Actions minutes, and a Claude subscription token. The workflow refuses to run if a pay-per-use API key exists.
    </p>

    <h2 id="standards">5. Editorial standards</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Claims trace to primary sources, listed under every module and signal. Third-party estimates are labeled as such.</li>
      <li>Every module shows its update date.</li>
      <li>Where experts disagree, the disagreement is presented, not resolved by fiat.</li>
    </ul>

